-- Chess, used here purely as a teaching example. Every other example in
-- this repo is a real business domain; this one is deliberately a game,
-- because chess makes one idea easy to see.
--
-- That idea: some rules are about what a board position is allowed to
-- look like, while others are about how the board is allowed to *change*
-- from one move to the next. "At most one king per colour" is the first
-- kind - you can check it by looking at a single snapshot of the board.
-- But "every move advances the move counter by exactly one", "the turn
-- always flips to the other player", and "a move captures at most one
-- piece" are the second kind. You cannot check them by looking at one
-- position alone; you have to compare the board before the move with the
-- board after it. That is what makes them interesting.
--
-- Morpholog expresses the second kind with `pre(...)`. Read
-- `pre(MoveCount(m))` as "the move count as it was before this move";
-- the unwrapped `MoveCount(n)` is the value after. A rule that mentions
-- both can constrain how one becomes the other.
--
-- The terms used below. An `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all
-- admitted state - the runtime refuses to commit any change that would
-- break one. A `require` is a gate on a single move, checked at the
-- moment the move is made. And the runtime stores `claims` it has agreed
-- to admit, never bare "facts": the board is just a set of claims that
-- such-and-such piece sits on such-and-such square.
--
-- This is not a chess engine. It does not enforce legal moves (a bishop
-- may slide sideways here), and it knows nothing of check, castling, or
-- promotion. It models only enough to show before-and-after rules.
-- Inspired by Murat Demirbas's Chess invariants post:
-- https://muratbuffalo.blogspot.com/2026/05/chess-invariants.html

program chess_transition_invariants

-- The vocabulary. The board is just a pile of "this piece is on this
-- square" claims, plus three counters that track the game as a whole:
-- how many moves have been played, how many pieces remain on the board,
-- and whose turn it is.
predicate PieceAt(square: Subject, piece_type: Subject, color: Subject)
predicate MoveCount(n: Decimal)
predicate PieceCount(n: Decimal)
predicate CurrentTurn(color: Subject)

-- Intents are messages announced to the outside world after a move
-- commits: the game started, a piece moved, a piece was captured.
intent GameStarted()
intent PieceMoved(src: Subject, dst: Subject)
intent PieceCaptured(src: Subject, dst: Subject, captured_type: Subject)

-- Rules about a single board position. Each one can be judged by
-- looking at one snapshot; none needs to know what the board looked
-- like a move ago.

-- A square holds at most one piece. If two claims put a piece on the
-- same square, they must be describing the very same piece.
invariant at_most_one_piece_per_square:
  (PieceAt(sq, t_a, c_a) and PieceAt(sq, t_b, c_b)) implies ((t_a = t_b) and (c_a = c_b))

-- Exactly one king of each colour - always. `sum(1 | PieceAt(_, #king,
-- #white))` counts how many white-king claims are on the board (it adds
-- 1 for every match), and we insist that count is 1. This is stronger
-- than "at most one king": it also forbids the count dropping to zero,
-- so a king can never be captured. We state it once per colour, since
-- chess has exactly two.
invariant exactly_one_white_king:
  sum(1 | PieceAt(_, #king, #white)) = 1

invariant exactly_one_black_king:
  sum(1 | PieceAt(_, #king, #black)) = 1

-- The piece counter must agree with the board it summarises: the number
-- stored in PieceCount must equal the actual number of pieces, counted
-- straight from the PieceAt claims. This stops the hand-kept counter
-- ever drifting from reality - admit a stray piece without bumping the
-- counter and this rule turns the whole move away.
invariant piece_count_matches_board:
  PieceCount(n) implies (n = sum(1 | PieceAt(_, _, _)))

-- At most eight pawns per colour. Here `c` is bound by the left side to
-- whichever colour has a pawn, and the count on the right is scoped to
-- that same colour. (Pawns only ever leave the board in this model, so
-- the bound is never really threatened - it is here to show counting
-- inside a comparison.)
invariant at_most_eight_pawns_per_color:
  PieceAt(_, #pawn, c) implies (sum(1 | PieceAt(_, #pawn, c)) <= 8)

-- Rules about how the board changes from one move to the next. These
-- compare the board after the move (the plain reading) with the board
-- before it (the `pre(...)` reading). You could not state them by
-- looking at a single position.

-- Every move bumps the move counter up by exactly one. The new count
-- must equal the previous count plus one.
invariant move_count_strictly_increases:
  (MoveCount(n) and pre(MoveCount(m))) implies (n = (m + 1))

-- The turn always flips: whoever is to move now must be the opposite
-- colour to whoever was to move before.
invariant turn_alternates:
  (CurrentTurn(t_now) and pre(CurrentTurn(t_prev))) implies (t_now != t_prev)

-- A single move captures at most one piece. The piece count after a
-- move is either unchanged (a quiet move) or exactly one less than
-- before (a capture). It can never drop by two.
invariant single_capture_per_move:
  (PieceCount(after) and pre(PieceCount(before))) implies ((after = before) or (after = (before - 1)))

-- The ways the board may change. A move proposes a set of changes;
-- the runtime applies them only if every invariant still holds.
-- `admit` adds a claim, `retract` removes one, `require` gates the move.

-- Set up the opening position: all 32 pieces on their starting squares,
-- the move counter at zero, the piece count at 32, white to play.
transformation start_game():
  admit PieceAt(#a1, #rook, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#b1, #knight, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#c1, #bishop, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#d1, #queen, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#e1, #king, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#f1, #bishop, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#g1, #knight, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#h1, #rook, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#a2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#b2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#c2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#d2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#e2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#f2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#g2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#h2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(#a7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#b7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#c7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#d7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#e7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#f7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#g7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#h7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#a8, #rook, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#b8, #knight, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#c8, #bishop, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#d8, #queen, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#e8, #king, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#f8, #bishop, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#g8, #knight, #black)
  admit PieceAt(#h8, #rook, #black)
  admit MoveCount(0)
  admit PieceCount(32)
  admit CurrentTurn(#white)
  emit GameStarted()

-- A move that does not capture. The piece leaves its source square and
-- lands on an empty destination. The gates check the basics of turn
-- order before anything moves: the piece being moved must belong to the
-- player whose turn it is, the next turn must be the other player, and
-- the destination must be empty. The move counter steps up by one; the
-- turn flips; the piece count is untouched.
transformation quiet_move(src, dst, new_turn):
  bind PieceAt(src, piece_type, piece_color)
  bind CurrentTurn(current_turn)
  bind MoveCount(m)
  require piece_color = current_turn
  require new_turn != current_turn
  require not (exists anything: PieceAt(dst, any_type, any_color))
  let next_m = m + 1
  retract PieceAt(src, piece_type, piece_color)
  retract CurrentTurn(current_turn)
  retract MoveCount(m)
  admit PieceAt(dst, piece_type, piece_color)
  admit CurrentTurn(new_turn)
  admit MoveCount(next_m)
  emit PieceMoved(src, dst)

-- A move that captures. Like a quiet move, but the destination holds an
-- enemy piece, which is removed from the board. The gates additionally
-- check that the captured piece really belongs to the opponent. The
-- piece count drops by exactly one.
transformation capturing_move(src, dst, new_turn):
  bind PieceAt(src, piece_type, piece_color)
  bind PieceAt(dst, captured_type, captured_color)
  bind CurrentTurn(current_turn)
  bind MoveCount(m)
  bind PieceCount(p)
  require piece_color = current_turn
  require new_turn != current_turn
  require captured_color != current_turn
  let next_m = m + 1
  let next_p = p - 1
  retract PieceAt(src, piece_type, piece_color)
  retract PieceAt(dst, captured_type, captured_color)
  retract CurrentTurn(current_turn)
  retract MoveCount(m)
  retract PieceCount(p)
  admit PieceAt(dst, piece_type, piece_color)
  admit CurrentTurn(new_turn)
  admit MoveCount(next_m)
  admit PieceCount(next_p)
  emit PieceCaptured(src, dst, captured_type)
